    "q-164384": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164384",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+(z-1)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+(z-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
